## Methods on Enums

You can define methods on enums just like structs. Self-receiving methods work on any variant, making enums as powerful as structs for organizing behavior.

### Defining Enum Methods

```xiom
pub type Shape = enum {
  Circle(radius: Float64),
  Rect(w: Float64, h: Float64),
}

fn Shape.area(self: &Shape) -> Float64 {
  match self {
    Circle(radius) => { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }
    Rect(w, h) => { return w * h; }
  }
}
```

### Calling Enum Methods

```xiom
let c = Shape.Circle(5.0);
let r = Shape.Rect(4.0, 6.0);
let a1 = c.area();  // 78.53...
let a2 = r.area();  // 24.0
```

### Method on Simple Enums

```xiom
pub type TrafficLight = enum {
  Red, Yellow, Green,
}

fn TrafficLight.next(self: &TrafficLight) -> TrafficLight {
  match self {
    Red => { return TrafficLight.Green; }
    Yellow => { return TrafficLight.Red; }
    Green => { return TrafficLight.Yellow; }
  }
}

fn TrafficLight.duration(self: &TrafficLight) -> Int {
  match self {
    Red => { return 30; }
    Yellow => { return 5; }
    Green => { return 25; }
  }
}
```

### Mutable Methods

```xiom
pub type Counter = enum {
  Active(count: Int),
  Paused(count: Int),
}

fn Counter.increment(self: &mut Counter) -> Unit {
  match self {
    Active(count) => { *count = *count + 1; }
    Paused(count) => { /* paused: ignore increment */ }
  }
}
```

### Method Chaining

```xiom
let result = Shape.Circle(5.0).area();
```

